1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,719
Every Linux machine starts the same way.

2
00:00:02,739 --> 00:00:04,942
Before the system, before any service,

3
00:00:04,962 --> 00:00:09,737
a small program called the bootloader wakes up, and decides which kernel to start,

4
00:00:09,757 --> 00:00:13,948
and with which options. Change it, and you change how the machine boots.

5
00:00:14,418 --> 00:00:17,065
In this video: modify the system bootloader.

6
00:00:21,585 --> 00:00:24,168
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

7
00:00:24,638 --> 00:00:26,367
Modify the system bootloader.

8
00:00:26,837 --> 00:00:31,041
We'll change how long the boot menu waits, and add an argument for the kernel.

9
00:00:37,561 --> 00:00:40,097
The bootloader on RHEL is G R U B two.

10
00:00:40,117 --> 00:00:43,998
Its settings live in slash E T C slash default slash grub.

11
00:00:44,018 --> 00:00:47,226
But the machine does not read that file at boot.

12
00:00:47,246 --> 00:00:52,069
It reads a generated file, slash boot slash grub two slash grub dot cfg.

13
00:00:52,089 --> 00:00:56,037
So there are two steps: edit the settings, then regenerate.

14
00:01:03,507 --> 00:01:05,326
Kernel arguments are different.

15
00:01:05,346 --> 00:01:08,766
The tool grubby changes them in the boot entries directly,

16
00:01:08,786 --> 00:01:10,783
for one kernel or for all of them.

17
00:01:16,253 --> 00:01:21,993
This lab is a U E F I virtual machine. Nothing is changed silently before the demo.

18
00:01:28,513 --> 00:01:30,986
First, look. Grep prints matching lines.

19
00:01:31,006 --> 00:01:36,096
We look for the grub timeout, and the kernel arguments. The timeout is one second:

20
00:01:36,116 --> 00:01:38,402
that is how long the boot menu waits.

21
00:01:38,422 --> 00:01:42,765
The kernel arguments include the console, and the crash kernel memory.

22
00:01:51,235 --> 00:01:56,015
Grubby dash dash default dash kernel prints which kernel starts by default.

23
00:01:56,035 --> 00:01:58,831
Sudo, because the boot files belong to root.

24
00:02:05,351 --> 00:02:09,586
Now change the timeout. Sed dash i edits the file in place.

25
00:02:09,606 --> 00:02:13,769
S, slash, pattern, slash, new, slash, replaces text. Here:

26
00:02:13,789 --> 00:02:18,889
the line that starts with GRUB timeout becomes GRUB timeout equals ten.

27
00:02:18,909 --> 00:02:20,115
Grep confirms it.

28
00:02:29,585 --> 00:02:32,351
But the machine does not use this file yet.

29
00:02:32,371 --> 00:02:36,562
Grub two dash mkconfig dash o writes the real configuration file.

30
00:02:44,082 --> 00:02:45,451
Now a kernel argument.

31
00:02:45,471 --> 00:02:48,420
Grubby dash dash update dash kernel equals all,

32
00:02:48,440 --> 00:02:53,345
dash dash args equals audit equals one. Update all kernels, add this argument.

33
00:02:53,365 --> 00:02:54,545
Nothing is printed.

34
00:02:54,565 --> 00:02:58,713
Grubby dash dash info equals default shows the default boot entry:

35
00:02:58,733 --> 00:03:01,555
the args line now ends with audit equals one.

36
00:03:13,075 --> 00:03:16,511
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

37
00:03:18,031 --> 00:03:23,602
We reboot. Slash proc slash cmdline shows the arguments the running kernel received:

38
00:03:23,622 --> 00:03:29,059
audit equals one is there. And the generated file now says set timeout equals ten.

39
00:03:29,079 --> 00:03:32,454
We show only those two lines of the timeout output.

40
00:03:42,974 --> 00:03:47,899
Your turn. Three things. Set the grub timeout to seven in the settings file.

41
00:03:47,919 --> 00:03:50,111
Regenerate the grub configuration.

42
00:03:50,131 --> 00:03:54,341
And add the kernel argument log level equals five to all kernels.

43
00:03:54,361 --> 00:03:57,269
Run the checker, then reboot and check again.

44
00:04:05,739 --> 00:04:07,489
Try before you open the solution.

45
00:04:13,009 --> 00:04:17,405
Next up: configure I P v four and I P v six addresses.

46
00:04:17,875 --> 00:04:20,778
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
